  "live_records": {
    "q-80190": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80190",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273827/assets/38fb409178372664f9ad19cb15a58b0307d15333e086702517f237cd3bdb260a.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=2{x}^{2}-2$ and $y=1-{x}^{2},$ shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*",
      "worked_solution": "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273827/assets/c6390f35fb796517054a15f16ee12f1354fa04f315f3cf1d91d498f19e73e15b.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=2{x}^{2}-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=1-{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(1-{x}^{2})-(2{x}^{2}-2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-3{x}^{2}+3)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
