    "q-11314": {
      "dom_id": "step-q11314",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273827/assets/4a72b5f351b7aa30015478d02e3edb817b913596f0757caec70ed91a28829e2d.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded between $g(x)=2-{x}^{2}-2x$ and $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x-1,$ shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*",
      "worked_solution": "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-{x}^{2}-2x & =2{x}^{2}+6x-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+8x-3 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{{8}^{2}-4\\cdot 3\\cdot (-3)}}{2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm 10}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-3$ to $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273827/assets/abfea1f4299a0e8ca980e817fda49f0d125cc11cbaae7dae85e5deca7d513c9f.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=2{x}^{2}+6x-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2-{x}^{2}-2x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1/3}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1/3}[\\,(2-{x}^{2}-2x)-(2{x}^{2}+6x-1)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1/3}(-3{x}^{2}-8x+3)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-11314-a",
