    "q-11582": {
      "dom_id": "step-q11582",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded between $y={x}^{2}-4x+3$ and $y=x-1?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273827/assets/c286710c66df6613cf448089a76902e63ed4783d31a89c728d5022a382ce3ddf.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+3 & =x-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=1$ to $x=4.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273827/assets/af15b99ba9e1233c76b6089fb1b4fb6cb62b9edcbedc7f0a241212075be3d33c.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-4x+3,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x-1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}[\\,(x-1)-({x}^{2}-4x+3)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(-{x}^{2}+5x-4)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
