    "q-3282": {
      "dom_id": "step-q3282",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Calculate the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y=-{x}^{2}-2$ and $y=-x-2.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a sketch:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273827/assets/af2b16006d5616854a3fb4e5f116c84375ee78f633ab8f313045b3d6feed922b.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-2 & =-x-2 \\\\ x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(1-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273827/assets/bf320ce6791c3f776961fee7fe3b7714895a3f5e38d8d721fd653ee816eb8997.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-x-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=-{x}^{2}-2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(-{x}^{2}-2)-(-x-2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3})-(0-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
